A||N||20||MickeyAsh||Future N* conversion||||5||
Z||000000||MickeyAsh||07-23-2001||11:43 PM||||Hey guys, I'm seriously considering the Northstar conversion for sometime in the near future... if someone can point me in the direction of a nice walkthrough or general N* site that can give me an idea of things I need before the project is started, I'd appreciate it. Obviously I need an engine, but I don't know anything about the Tranny or anything like that... do I have to change the stock 86 Auto Tranny and put in a caddy transmission or what? I plan to buy the ECm and harness from chrfab or another vendor if I find one before the project begins... somebody give me a list of major things to consider besides all that...  ::laughing at the vagueness of that post:: TIA guys.||||reg||5
Z||000001||FieroLisa||07-24-2001||12:53 AM||||Well this has all been discussed extensively, so my advice to you would be to use the search function on the forum and type in Northstar, you'll get a lot of answer there, since this has been debated so many times here by now. The people I know who do the Northstars are Chris Moore (Northstar87 on the forum), John Norman (not on forum), Jay, and umm another company whose name is slipping my mind since its 1 am. <P>Tranny is a huge debate too, most of the existing ones use the stock Caddy tranmission for reliability but there are people experimenting with manuals.  Someday when a 6 speed is paired with the N*, I will invest.<P>------------------<BR><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/fierolisa.gif">  <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/" TARGET=_blank>FieroLisa's Website</A>*<A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ncfieros/" TARGET=_blank>NC Tarheels Fieros Site</A><P>AIM: FieroLisa||||reg||1
Z||000002||Jay||07-24-2001||12:16 PM||||Hey Mickey, I've got a Northstar and I love it.  You can see the basics of the conversion on my site at <A HREF="http://www.members.home.net/jaygrande" TARGET=_blank>www.members.home.net/jaygrande</A>  and if you got any questions just ask, there are a few Northstars on the board and we will all be glad to help.  BTW mine is for sale!<P>------------------<BR>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT<BR>www.members.home.net/jaygrande<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/cayuga3_3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Adrift||07-24-2001||05:14 PM||||Jay, what kind of 0-60 and 1/4 mile times do you get on your northstar?<BR>How much did it cost you?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Will||07-24-2001||06:51 PM||||I'm in the middle of N* swap using a Getrag 5 spd. I'm going to initially try a '95 Cadillac computer with a '95 Aurora PROM and fuel pressure turned up to 57 psi (engine has adjustable FPR from factory). I'm going to try the V6 radiator for now and see how it does.<P>Costs:<BR>'95 Northstar engine, complete except for A/C compressor and P/S pump:<BR>$2000<P>Getrag transmisison from Beretta, including extension shaft and axles:<BR>$150 (outstanding deal!)<P>Extra differential side bearings from Rodney Dickman:<BR>$80 for two<P>Internal tranmission bearings (input shaft right side bearing, output shaft R/S bearing, both diff side bearings)<BR>~$60<P>Torsen LSD:<BR>$1500<P>'95 Aurora PROM:<BR>~$30<P>Extra cradle for assembly:<BR>$60<P>'96 Corvette fuel pump:<BR>~$100 (used to be 85  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> )<P>CenterForce dual friction clutch for modified V6 flywheel:<BR>~$285<P>Project car ('87 GT fully optioned with ~130K):<BR>$700<P>Still to come:<BR>Rodney Dickman decklid strut kit.<BR>New (OE rubber--see how long they last) trans and engine mounts<BR>Exhaust system<BR>An Arseload of $0.05 and $0.10 stuff.<P><BR>A lot of expenses, like modifying the V6 flywheel, modifying the transmission case, and modifying the engine block to short belt it using the stock idler (don't know if that will work yet) I did myself and saved a load of $$$.<BR>The toolset to set preload on the diff side bearings is ~$700 (!!!) from GM. Consequently I'm making my own.<P>Search under Northstar and my user name. I've made a few posts about it in the past.<P>If you're going to use an Auto, by all means use the Caddy auto. It's a heavy pig but it is certainly strong enough and it makes the swap much simpler.<P>No thorough Northstar swap site exists to my knowledge.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jay||07-25-2001||02:31 PM||||Adrift, I'm running consistantly in the mid 13's at about 105mph.  I'm going to the track in a few weeks and I'll let everyone know what I run.  I did most of the work myself like Will except I used the automatic tranny from the STS.  The engine cost me $1600 Canadian including the tranny and harness but I converted it from a 97 to a 95 and am using a 94 prom.<P>------------------<BR>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT<BR>www.members.home.net/jaygrande<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/cayuga3_3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Jay||07-25-2001||02:57 PM||||Will, I shortened my belt also, but I believe I used the pulley from a 3800 engine on the Cadillac tensioner.  However I did rotate the tensioner to a position that it will push on the belt.  When you line it all up you'll see what I mean.  I removed the AC and power steerning pullies all together.  Noticed a difference, less drap from the accessories.||||reg||1
Z||000007||EdsB52||07-25-2001||03:26 PM||||Look at this site, Kevin is pretty cool and will give you information and advice.<P><BR> <A HREF="http://www.design1systems.com/northstar/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.design1systems.com/northstar/index.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Will||07-25-2001||03:47 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by EdsB52:<BR><B>Look at this site, Kevin is pretty cool and will give you information and advice.<P><BR> <A HREF="http://www.design1systems.com/northstar/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.design1systems.com/northstar/index.html</A> </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I've looked at the pictures in their photo gallery. It looks to me like they make the conversion 5 or 6 times as much work as it has to be, and remove half your trunk while they're at it.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Will||07-25-2001||07:47 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jay:<BR><B>Will, I shortened my belt also, but I believe I used the pulley from a 3800 engine on the Cadillac tensioner.  However I did rotate the tensioner to a position that it will push on the belt.  When you line it all up you'll see what I mean.  I removed the AC and power steerning pullies all together.  Noticed a difference, less drap from the accessories.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I'm mounting the idler in one of the now unused P/S pump holes so that it pulls up. I left the A/C and Alt as stock. The belt goes up from the crank, over the idler/tensioner, then down and to the right (facing front of engine) over the Alt and A/C compressor.<P>How exactly is yours run<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 07-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||BigTurbo87GT||07-27-2001||03:22 PM||||Hey Will, How hard is it to use the Cadillac AC compressor with the Fiero lines? I was just gonna live without AC but if it's not too much hassle I would rather have it. My motor is bare so I need to buy brackets and a compressor, which year/model (if any) is better. My engine is a 96 STS, my car is an 87Gt.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Will||07-27-2001||11:35 PM||||Actually, I think the fiero V6 compressor can be used if you take it apart and rotate the housing 90 or 180 degrees then put it back together. Since a compressor didn't come with my engine, but the bracket did, I'me just going to reuse the Fiero compressor.<P>I have a '95. I think that accessory mounting was the same for all models and all years until they went to direct mount accessories for, I think, MY2K.||||reg||1
Z||000012||BigTurbo87GT||07-30-2001||11:55 AM||||Sweet, I'm still trying to find brackets and pulleys and such. It seems very few junkyards carry this stuff (or are not willing to part a motor).||||reg||1
Z||000013||Will||07-30-2001||04:10 PM||||I just discovered today that it may be necessary to use the Fiero A/C compressor with the Cadillac. The Fiero and Cadillac compressors use three mounting holes which are spaced farther apart than the more common three hole pattern. The junkyard I checked today doesn't seem to have any of the larger pattern compressors.<BR>Also, the holes in the Fiero compressor need to have the threads drilled out of them in order to bolt to the Cadillac bracket. That can be done while the compressor is apart to have the housing rotated 90 degrees.<BR>Using the Fiero compressor hoses also means that the Northstar oil cooler lines will have to be changed from sticking out several inches from the filter bracket to elbows threaded directly into the filter bracket. This shoudl be trivial.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000014||Will||07-30-2001||10:51 PM||||Oops.<BR>Belay my last.<P>All the spread bolt patterns are the same. You will need a compressor with four mounting lugs instead of three, and the largest six rib pulley you can find. Once you have this, drill its mounting holes out to 13/32 and bolt it onto the engine.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||artherd||07-31-2001||11:25 PM||||I'm actually going to a wicked-cool setup I first saw on an electric car (modified sunbird I belive it was?)<P>I am relocating the compressor up front along with the rest of the system, and I will power it from (drumrool please..... ok, so you guessed it by now. an electric motor!)<P>I am also not-so-idly thinking about an electronic vacumm pump for the brake power assist. (ok, three pumps, for tripple redundancy :)<P>Advantages: no more drag on the engine 24/7, but slightly less total effiency when teh AC is on.<P>Also, I'm going to need one heckuva large alternator. Not sure where to source yet. I am talking in the range of 200+A...<P>I want nothing on the crank but an alternator, and nothing on vacumm at all, we will see!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000016||Formula||08-01-2001||03:07 PM||||it might be a good idea to put a water pump on the engine too.  I don't think the electric ones are all that great for a lot of commuting.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Formula||08-01-2001||03:10 PM||||your going to need vacuum for the pvc from at least the air cleaner, like the normal fiero setup.  you might run inot problems with the northstar and all the sensors by getting rid of all the emmisions controlled vacuum stuff... just my opinion but it would be sweet if you could do it.||||reg||1
Z||000018||rockcrawl||08-01-2001||07:23 PM||||Ben,<BR> Many of the Cadillacs use an electric vacuum pump. My '87 Deville had one and my '91 Deville has one. They stick it way down in the front of the fender. The MAP, EGR, PCV, FPR and other emissions stuff still uses engine vacuum, they have to in order to work properly. The brake booster and climate controls use the "artificial" vacuum. I'm not sure if or how the two vacuum systems are intertwined. I'd imagine they need to be totally separate to get proper operation of the MAP and FPR.||||reg||1
Z||000019||EdsB52||08-01-2001||08:17 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>I'm actually going to a wicked-cool setup I first saw on an electric car (modified sunbird I belive it was?)<P>I am relocating the compressor up front along with the rest of the system, and I will power it from (drumrool please..... ok, so you guessed it by now. an electric motor!)<P>I am also not-so-idly thinking about an electronic vacumm pump for the brake power assist. (ok, three pumps, for tripple redundancy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Advantages: no more drag on the engine 24/7, but slightly less total effiency when teh AC is on.<P>Also, I'm going to need one heckuva large alternator. Not sure where to source yet. I am talking in the range of 200+A...<P>I want nothing on the crank but an alternator, and nothing on vacumm at all, we will see!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Do a search, because I proposed this a year ago and someone came on to mathematically refute the possibility of it. They figured I would need an alternator that put out some ungodly amount of amps, as well as an electric motor that was rated at such a large # of HP that it would also be quite large. I actually like the idea of it and if you could find a way to reasonably do it, you could make money off the kits. I think DC motors are quite expensive. The advantages are numerous though. The A/C runs at a constant rpm, the A/C is in a cool environment. With the battery up front, the electric motor wouldn't have a lot of drop. The A/C components are all up front anyway, so it consolidates the loop. That actaully might be bad though, as you would have less freon to circulate??? I'm not an A/C specialist, but I think it's possible to not have enough fluid (freon) capacity in the system. Good luck, keep us informed.||||reg||1
Z||000020||artherd||08-02-2001||03:56 AM||||Rockcrawl; THANK YOU for the tip!!  I will post what results (hopefully totally fluxulation-free brakes :) <P><BR>The Northstar has a nice little cam driven integral water pump (pretty cool actually.)   Of course PCV/engine/ETC type equipment has to connect to the intake, but I wouldn't consider those "accessories" rather the engine as a system.  I will likely retain ALL emmisions gear. :)<P>My goal is to get rid of vacumm and AC lines running from the engine to the car (and their associated loads, and innefficencies and mess :)<P>Should be doable, and Ed, I've seen the motor/compressor setup before; it works.  I WILL need a huge alternator though. or possibly two. (which might offset any actual performance gains, but I'm going mostly for cleanness of install here.)<P>The motors will not be cheap, and the wire will be large. I will be using a 10HP brushed permanant rare-earth magnet high-torque motor. It will not be cheap :(<P>But no AC lines to worry about when droping the cradle? (or in my engine swap, ever?) cool! :)<P><BR>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
